            <ArticleTitle>Obesity and endometrial hyperplasia and cancer in premenopausal women: A systematic review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>689.e1-689.e17</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ajog.2016.01.175</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0002-9378(16)00225-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To systematically review the literature on the association between obesity and endometrial hyperplasia or cancer in premenopausal women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SOURCES" NlmCategory="METHODS">We searched the bibliographic databases MEDLINE, EMBASE, PubMed, and CINAHL (inception to May 5, 2015), and checked reference lists of included studies and systematic reviews.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY ELIGIBILITY CRITERIA" NlmCategory="METHODS">Studies of more than 50 women with endometrial pathology diagnosed during premenopause that reported on obesity as a risk factor were eligible.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY APPRAISAL AND SYNTHESIS METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Study identification and data extraction were independently performed by 2 authors. Where possible, data were pooled in a generic inverse variance forest plot. Heterogeneity was reported using the I(2) statistic.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Nine case-control studies of moderate quality were included. Quantitative analysis of 5 studies showed a dose-response relationship of body mass index and increased risk of endometrial cancer. For studies of women with body mass index of ≥25, the pooled odds ratio was 3.85 (95% confidence interval 2.53-5.84); body mass index of ≥30 was 5.25 (4.00-6.90); and body mass index of ≥40 was 19.79 (11.18-35.03).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Body mass index is a consistent and leading risk factor for endometrial complex hyperplasia or cancer in premenopausal women. Body mass index should be considered when deciding to assess the endometrium in symptomatic premenopausal women.</AbstractText>
